ng to remove significant chemical or heat-damaged lengths and begin or reset a natural-hair routine.","photoBrief":"Subject: Big Chop — removal of relaxed, chemically processed or severely damaged lengths so the client’s natural curl or coil texture remains.\nRequired visible features: clearly natural textured hair remaining after the cut; short natural curls or coils; ideally cutting, transition, or before/after context showing processed lengths being removed.\nComposition: Show the natural-texture transition clearly enough to distinguish the service from an ordinary short haircut. Use an authentic African salon/client context and keep the remaining natural texture visible.\nAvoid: ordinary pixie cuts, fades, trims, generic short hairstyles, relaxed short hair, wigs, or any image where the defining texture transition cannot be established.\nQualification: Match the exact Big Chop service identity; the visible evidence must support processed-hair removal and retained natural texture."},"Natural Hair Styling|Defined Wash & Go":{"description":"Defined Wash & Go is a natural-hair styling service that enhances and showcases the client’s existing curl or coil pattern without extensions or a set created by braids, twists or rods. The hair is cleansed, conditioned and detangled, defining products are applied while the hair is wet, and the curls are shaped and dried by air or controlled diffusion. What makes it distinctive is that the service defines the client’s natural texture rather than creating a new wave or curl pattern. Clients receive hydrated, defined, lower-frizz curls or coils with a lightweight natural finish and relevant product or maintenance guidance. It suits natural hair with an existing curl or coil pattern, especially clients wanting everyday definition without extensions or permanent texture change.","photoBrief":"Subject: Defined Wash & Go — a finished natural-hair result with the client’s own curls or coils visibly defined and clumped.\nRequired visible features: natural curls/coils, clear curl definition, hydrated lightweight finish, and no extension-created pattern.\nComposition: Show enough of the finished hair to verify the natural curl pattern and definition on an African client; the final result should be the main subject rather than a shampoo-basin scene.\nAvoid: braid-outs, twist-outs, rod sets, braids, twists, relaxed hair, undefined wet hair, or generic curly portraits where a defined Wash & Go cannot be established.\nQualification: The image must show the client’s natural curl or coil pattern being defined rather than a new pattern created by another styling method."},"Natural Hair Styling|Detangling":{"description":"Detangling is the careful separation of knots and interlocked loose strands. The stylist works through manageable sections with suitable slip and tools, easing tangles while controlling pulling. Its distinctive focus is removing tangles rather than creating a new hairstyle. Clients receive more separated and manageable hair ready for the next care step. It suits clients whose loose hair is tangled or preparing for washing or styling.","photoBrief":"Subject: Detangling — the careful separation of knots and interlocked loose strands.\nRequired visible features: sectioned textured hair; controlled detangling with hands or suitable tools.\nComposition: Show the actual care procedure in a clean African salon, with the relevant hair or scalp and the stylist’s hands clearly visible. A finished-hair portrait alone cannot demonstrate that this treatment was performed.\nAvoid: braid installation; cutting away substantial hair presented as detangling; a polished portrait without process.\nQualification: Match this exact service or style; do not approve a photo merely because it belongs to the same category. A photo cannot prove product identity, hair material, geographical coverage or package entitlements; verify those from the source where relevant."},"Natural Hair Styling|Finger Coils":{"descriptio