lishes the sectioning and foundation for a client’s long-term loc journey. The stylist maps a clean grid and forms each section using an appropriate starter technique such as coils, twists or interlocking according to the client’s hair and chosen loc plan. What makes it distinctive is that the service creates the structural beginning of future locs, so section size, grid consistency and foundation technique matter more than a mature-loc appearance. Clients receive a neat, uniform starter-loc foundation together with practical guidance on early-stage care and maintenance. It suits clients who are ready to begin a loc journey and want a professionally planned, consistent foundation.","photoBrief":"Subject: Starter Locs — newly established loc foundations with a clean, consistent grid and early-stage loc formation.\nRequired visible features: fresh sectioning/grid; newly formed coils/twists/interlocked starter bases; clear early-stage rather than mature-loc appearance.\nComposition: Show the scalp/grid and enough of the starter formation to verify the service stage on an African client.\nAvoid: mature locs, loc extensions, generic twists presented as established starter locs without visible grid context, or retwist-only imagery.\nQualification: Match new loc foundation + clean grid + early-stage loc formation."},"Weaving & Extensions|Clip-In Installation":{"description":"Clip-In Installation is the fitting and blending of removable clip-in extensions. The stylist sections the hair, positions the clip-in pieces and blends their placement with the natural hair. Its distinctive focus is a clip-attached system intended to be removable rather than sewn or bonded in. Clients receive a fitted clip-in look with the agreed extra length or volume. It suits clients wanting temporary extension wear.","photoBrief":"Subject: Clip-In Installation — the fitting and blending of removable clip-in extensions.\nRequired visible features: clip-in wefts or attachment evidence; blended insta